Git Rebase

The git rebase command takes the commits from your branch and replays them, one by one, on top of another branch.

Say you made two commits on your branch, and meanwhile a teammate added new commits to main. Rebase takes your two commits off their old parent and creates them again, in the same order, with the new tip of main as the starting point. The changes inside each commit are the same, but the commits themselves are new, and your branch pointer now sits on top of the latest main.

main old tip
     |
     |
main new tip
     |
     |
your commit 1'
     |
     |
your commit 2'

You run rebase from the branch you want to move:
git switch feature
git rebase main


After this, git log on feature shows your rebased commits first because they are newest, with the latest main commits below them. There is no merge commit. The history is one straight line. People call this a linear history, and it is easier to read than a history full of merge bubbles.

The simple test before you rebase
Ask one question: has anyone else pulled these commits?

Commits only on your machine, never pushed: rebase freely.
Commits pushed to a personal branch nobody pulled yet: usually still fine, but you must force push, and you should be sure you are the only user of that branch.
Commits on main or any branch teammates work on: never rebase them. Use git merge or git revert instead, because those add new commits without rewriting old ones.


Git Cherry-pick
Rebase and merge move a whole branch. Sometimes you want just one commit from it. A typical case: an experiment branch holds ten throwaway commits, but one of them is a real bug fix that main needs today. You do not want the experiments, only that one fix.

Cherry-pick copies exactly the one commit you name and leaves the rest of the branch where it is.

git switch main
git cherry-pick 4f8d0a3


 Like rebase, cherry-pick creates a new commit with the same changes and message but a different hash, because the parent is different. The original commit still exists on the experiment branch, untouched.

If the picked commit touches lines that differ on your branch, cherry-pick pauses exactly like a rebase. You fix the file, git add it, and run git cherry-pick --continue, or back out with git cherry-pick --abort.

Generate the token on GitHub
On GitHub, open Settings, then "Developer settings", then "Personal access tokens", then "Fine-grained tokens".
Click "Generate new token" and give it a name.
Set an expiry date. A short one is safer.
Under repository access, choose only your practice repositories, not all repositories.
Under permissions, give Contents read and write access. That is enough for ordinary push, pull, fetch, and clone. If you will change files under .github/workflows, also grant the fine-grained Workflows write permission.

