Remote and git clone

A remote is a saved address of another copy of your repository, usually one hosted on GitHub. Git stores the address inside your repository under a short name. Your repository knows the address, so you never have to type it again.

By convention the main remote is named origin. When you copy a repository from GitHub, Git creates this remote for you automatically.

Inside a cloned repository, git remote -v shows the saved addresses:

git remote -v

origin  https://github.com/octocat/Hello-World (fetch)
origin  https://github.com/octocat/Hello-World (push)


PUshing into Github

The git remote add command saves a remote address under a name:

bash

cd /root/git-journey
git remote add origin https://github.com/yourname/git-journey.git
Nothing is uploaded yet. This command only saves the address inside your local repository. No commits have traveled. Confirm the saved address with:

bash
git remote -v

The git ls-remote origin command contacts the remote and lists what it has. 


GIT PUSH
The git push command uploads your local commits to a remote repository. Until you push, every commit you make exists only on your machine. Pushing copies those commits to the repository on GitHub, so teammates can see them.

bash

git push origin main
This reads as: send my main branch to the remote named origin. Git uploads every commit the remote does not have yet and moves the remote's main to match yours.


On the first push of a branch, add -u:
git push -u origin main

The -u option records origin/main as the upstream of your local main: the remote branch it is paired with. After that, plain git push with no arguments knows where to go, and git status can tell you whether you are ahead of or behind the remote.


origin/main: your copy of the remote's position

Your repository holds a marker called origin/main. It records a locally known position of the remote's main; it does not update merely because a command contacts the remote.

The git fetch origin command refreshes these markers: it asks the remote what it has and downloads anything new, without touching your own branches. Fetch is how you check what the remote repository holds. A later topic covers fetch fully; for now you use it to verify that a push really arrived:


git fetch origin
git log --oneline origin/main -n 1
If that prints the same commit as your local main, your work is on GitHub.

When the push is rejected - 
Bring the remote commits into your local branch first, then push again. The commands for that (git pull, and rebasing on top of the remote) are the subject of the next topic. The order is always the same: download their work, combine it with yours locally, then push the result.

Dont do - git push --force origin main
