fetch vs pull: two ways to get new commits

When to use which
Use git fetch when you want to look before you touch anything: see what changed, compare, decide. Use git pull when you simply want to be up to date. Fetch is always safe. Pull changes your branch.

The git fetch command downloads new commits from a remote but does not touch your own branch. The git pull command downloads them and then integrates them into your current branch. It usually merges, but configuration can make it rebase or require you to choose when histories diverge. That difference matters every day in a team.

Why you need either one
Your repository on GitHub changes without you. A teammate pushes a commit, and the repository on GitHub now holds a commit that your local repository has never seen. Git never downloads anything by itself. You must ask for it.


GIT FETCH
git fetch: download without touching your branch

git fetch origin
git fetch downloads the new commits into your repository but does not apply them to your branch. The new commits are stored under the remote-tracking branch origin/main. Your own main branch does not move. Your working files do not change.


$ git fetch origin
remote: Enumerating objects: 5, done.
From github.com:someuser/git-journey
   a1b2c3d..e4f5a6b  main -> origin/main
The last line says: on GitHub, main moved from a1b2c3d to e4f5a6b, and my local origin/main now records that.

GIT PULL
git pull: download and integrate in one command
git pull origin main -This pull moves your branch forward


git pull origin main

